The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-22-spark-benchmark-setup-approved-plan.txt|approved plan]] creates [[Resources/Coder Prompts/spark-benchmark-setup]] for immediate direct-host Docker/Compose deployment. [[Resources/Coder Prompts/benchmark-runner]] retains case scheduling, comparisons and exports. Local inference is added only when needed. Practical storage headroom and owned cleanup apply now; M2 VMs, factories, registry and full capacity-controller services remain separate. This is a prompt/documentation delivery, not evidence that the Spark was accessed or qualified.

## Prior multi-system proposal: superseded

The earlier comparison proposal is retained in its original prompt captures and pre-edit snapshots. The current user direction below removes that delivery requirement and replaces it with one shared execution interface.

## Runner usability and CLI-first scope

The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-22-runner-cli-interface.txt|latest clarification and preference]] confirms an integrated runner package with a good interface and no collisions; the first version is CLI only. [[Resources/Coder Prompts/benchmark-runner]] now requires consistent commands, agent-readable JSON, helpful errors, an installed-command walkthrough and campaign ownership checks. Codex packaging remains target integration, not the runner deliverable. No web UI is requested for this version.

## Current runner scope: tasks on AI4Research

The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-22-ai4r-runner-scope.txt|latest correction]] narrows [[Resources/Coder Prompts/benchmark-runner]] to running tasks on AI4Research, collecting actual outputs/timing/usage and exporting evidence for human judgment. The CLI remains first. One documented versioned execution interface allows a hypothetical conforming system to work through configuration; no second product integration is required. The current coder prompts no longer prescribe a particular model provider or comparative target. [[Resources/Coder Prompts/spark-benchmark-setup]] deploys only AI4Research task instances. Earlier captures remain history, not current implementation scope.

## Exact AI4R container and file layout

The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-22-two-image-runner-layout.txt|latest exact layout]] fixes two images from AI4R: `ai4r` contains the app; `benchmark-runner` contains the runner CLI/queue/collection code and runs separately. User-supplied cases and inputs are mounted read-only into the runner; its persistent output bind mount points at the user's chosen host directory. The app receives only permitted task inputs and exposes actual artifact bytes through the shared interface. The runner automatically copies, verifies and records them on the mount; saved artifacts survive removal of both containers. App execution and collection have separate statuses. Files in container writable layers consume disk and are disposable, not automatically memory-only. Earlier host-installed-runner and replica requirements are superseded for this two-container deployment. Both coder prompts now state this explicitly. No Docker deployment was performed.

## JiuwenSwarm reuse review

[[Resources/Coder Prompts/benchmark-runner]] now identifies reusable app authentication/permission enforcement, sidecar client/ledger/evidence/cleanup and container settings. App policies stay in the app; the sidecar uses scoped client operations. Reviewed source is pinned in `Resources/review-batches/2026-10-22-runner-policy-reuse/source-receipt.json`. App-wheel inclusion of runner modules, required score mounts and bounded in-memory export helpers still need adaptation. These are source findings, not deployment results.

## Shared prompt, code execution and observability

The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-22-runner-code-observability.txt|latest direction]] makes the prompt explicitly for human and AI readers. [[Resources/Coder Prompts/benchmark-runner]] now requires declared executable steps and broad live/retained execution telemetry. Trusted runner setup/collection scripts run in owned sidecar workspaces; generated/untrusted task code uses the app execution boundary. Observations remain separate from human correctness assessment. Two-image deployment and persistent outputs are unchanged.

## Durable labs as emerging projects

The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-22-durable-startup-labs.txt|latest lab direction]] describes persistent labs as small startups/hackathon projects that accumulate data and capabilities, change models, undergo RSI and present results for future development. [[Designs/Durable Project Labs]] now owns this lifecycle. It supersedes disposable-lab framing: lab identity survives VM recovery, while deliberate forks get new identities. Broader candidate evolution, protected admission, consistent backup/restore and bounded storage are proposed M2 behavior; no infrastructure deployment or automatic activation is claimed. Immediate benchmark tooling remains separate.

## Current runner delivery: sequential control and data plane

The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-22-sequential-control-data-runner.txt|latest change]] prioritizes a small working app-testing tool. [[Resources/Coder Prompts/benchmark-runner]] is now one sequential control/data runner with one app container: it owns task sequencing, input transfer, execution observations and persistent artifact collection. Parallel execution, worker pools, leases and replicas are removed from first-delivery requirements. Existing code and essential telemetry are reused; advanced instrumentation is optional. The physical two-container separation remains, but the runner is an active campaign controller and data collector. Spark and current reading routes are aligned; M2 lab designs remain separate.

